## KEYWORDS
triboelectric nanogenerator, metal-organic frameworks, UiO-66, functional group, isostructural
## Introduction
The triboelectric nanogenerator (TENG) has attracted increasing attention due to its excellent electricity generation performance, which can directly convert mechanical energy into electric energy [1-5]. The working mechanism of TENG is based on the coupling effect of triboelectrification and electrostatic induction [6-9]. Up to now, relatively low output performance is still one important barrier to practical applications of TENG. Among the many approaches, such as improvement of surface roughness and optimization of surface topography, the reasonable selection of friction materials is an effective method to fundamentally solve the problem of low triboelectric performance.Works about triboelectric materials  mostly focus on traditional organic polymers,suchaspolydimethylsiloxane(PDMS)[5,7], polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE) [4]，and polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF) [9],which are restricted to the triboelectric series. It is urgently necessary to explore and enrich the other high performance triboelectric material.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a new class of inorganic-organic crystalline hybrid materials with networks containing metal nodes and organic linkers [10] and have great potential applications in gas sorption and storage [ll],catalysis [12], molecular sensing [13], and drug delivery [14]. The combination of a vast number of organic links and inorganic nodes generates thousands of novel MOF materials. Unlike other materials, MOFs have achieved oriented design synthesis by the rational design of inorganic secondary building units and organic linkers. Interestingly, MOFs can also be functionalized with various  functional  groups  through  in-situ  synthesis  or postsynthetic approaches to endow MOFs with particular chemical properties, without affecting the underlying topology [15]. So far, several MOFs have been reported in TENG, which demonstrate that MOFs are promising triboelectric materials [16-26]. The functional group is very important for the output performance of TENG since it can facilitate the gain/loss of charges [6]. Therefore, it is necessary to systematically research the effect of functional groups in the isostructural MOFs on the output performance of the TENG. To the best of our knowledge, there is no systematic research on functional groups of MOFs on the performance of the TENG. A $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOF material UiO-66 based on $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{12}$ secondary building units has outstanding physicochemical properties as well as high mechanical and thermal stability [27]. Furthermore, UiO-66 film has been successfully functionalized with several specialized functionalities to regulate its property and application [28].
Based on the above considerations, we choose the isostructural UiO-66-X $\mathrm{(X=H,}$ $\mathrm{NH}_{2},$ $\mathrm{NO}_{2},$ and Br) family as triboelectric materials to investigate the underlying relationships between diferent functional groups with the electron-donating $\left(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ 0r electron-withdrawing ability $\left(-\mathrm{NO}_{2}\right.$ and -Br) and triboelectric performance of TENG. The outperformance of TENG follows the order of $\mathrm{UiO-66\mathrm{-}N O_{2}\ >\ U i O-66\mathrm{-}B r\ >\ U i O-66\ >\ U i O-66\mathrm{-}N H_{2}},$ which is consistent with the order of the electron-withdrawing ability of four groups $\mathrm{-NO}_{2}>\mathrm{-Br}>\mathrm{-H}>\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ The mechanism is further confirmed by surface potential, binding energy, and theoretical calculations for the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO). Furthermore, the TENG exhibits good stability and durability. This work first demonstrates that the MOFs as triboelectric materials can regulate the output performance of MOF TENG by functionalizing with different functional groups, and will guide us on how to choose and design the high-efficiency triboelectric material MOFs to enhance the output performance of TENG.
## 2 Results and discussion
In order to systematically evaluate the potential influence of terephthalate functionalization of UiO-66 on the performance of the TENG, all the UiO-66-X films bearing different functional groups were designed as triboelectric layers to fabricate UiO-66-TENG. Figure 2(e) shows the schematic structure of the TENG based on UiO-66 film with a different functional group. The other triboelectric layer is a piece of copper foil adhered to an acrylic substrate. All TENG devices were fabricated by applying a typical vertical contact-separation mode. The basic working mechanism of the TENG is shown in Fig.S3 in the ESM. At the initial state, there is no charge generation on both the UiO-66-X films and Cu foil because of the absence of force. When fully pressed by applying the external mechanical force, equal opposite charges are generated both at the surfaces of the triboelectric layers. The electron transfers from Cu foil to UiO-66-X film due to their different charge affinities so that Cu foil is positively charged and UiO-66-X film is negatively charged. Upon release, an electrical potential difference between the two electrodes drives electrons to flow from the bottom electrode to the upper electrode through an external circuit. When fully released, the bottom electrode and UiO-66-X film have equal opposite charges, and then a new electrostatic equilibrium state is reached. On applying the force, the two triboelectric layers come into contact again, and the reversed potential difference between the two electrodes will build and push electron to flow from the upper electrode to the bottom electrode through an external circuit. Alternating output signals will be present in the TENG under periodical pressing and releasing conditions.
The triboelectric output signals, open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{OC}})_{:}$ short-circuit  current $(I_{\mathrm{SC}})$ 。 and transferred charge $(Q)$ of UiO-66-X TENG are shown in Figs. 3(a)-3(c). It is obviously seen that triboelectric properties significantly depend on the functional group. The parent UiO-66 TENG achieves a $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $7.46\mathrm{V}$ 。 $I_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $0.07~\upmu\mathrm{A},$ and $Q$ of $1.66~\mathrm{nC}.$ When functionalized with electronwithdrawing group $\begin{array}{r}{-\mathrm{NH}_{2},}\end{array}$ the triboelectric performance of TENG $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ decreases. While functionalized with electrondonating functional groups of $\left.-\mathrm{NO}_{2}\right.$ and -Br, the $V_{\mathrm{OC}},I_{\mathrm{SC}},$ and $Q$ of TENG increase compared to that of the UiO-66 TENG. The largest output voltage and current from UiO- $66\mathrm{-NO}_{2}$ TENG are about $23.79{\mathrm{~V~}}$ and $0.29~\upmu\mathrm{A},$ which are 3.l9 and 4.l4 times over that of the UiO-66 TENG, respectively. Figure 3(d) compares the triboelectric output performances of UiO-66-X TENG with various functional groups. Obviously, the sequence of functional groups for the generated output signals of TENG is ${\mathrm{-NO}}_{2}>{\mathrm{-Br}}>$ $\mathrm{\cdotH}>\mathrm{\-NH}_{2}$
The stability is an important evaluation index for the practical application of the UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNO}_{2}$ TENG. A test is conducted using a linear motor under room temperature (Fig. S4 in the ESM). There is no obvious change in the output performance even about 1,000 cycles after 10o days, which is attributed to the exceptionally high stability of UiO- $66\mathrm{-NO}_{2}$
As we all know, surface roughness is an important factor in the triboelectric performance of the TENG. Large surface roughness can increase the effective contact area of triboelectric material, thus improving the output performance of TENG. The corresponding three-dimensional (3D） height atomic force microscopy (AFM) images of four isostructural UiO-66-X are shown in Fig. S5 in the ESM. The root mean square (RMS) values of UiO-66, UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2},$ ${\mathrm{UiO}}{\cdot}66{\cdot}{\mathrm{NO}}_{2},$ and UiO-66-Br are 198,191,173, and $202\ \mathrm{~nm}.$ ， respectively, demonstrating that four isostructural UiO-66-X have similar surface roughness. The result means that difference in the surface roughness for four isostructural UiO-66-X is so small that the roughness has a negligible effect on the output performance of TENG. To further discuss how the functional group influences triboelectric performance of the TENG, the surface potential of a series of UiO-66 films grafted with different functional groups was examined. Figure 4(a) shows the temporal surface potential and Fig. $4(\mathrm{b})$ shows the surface potential versus the functional groups. The measured surface potentials of UiO-66, UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NO}_{2},$ and UiO-66-Br are $-6.30$ 。 $-2.30$ 。-17.85,and $-7.70\mathrm{V}_{:}$ 、respectively. The negative surface potential demonstrates that UiO-66, UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2},$ UiO- $66\mathrm{-NO}_{2},$ and UiO-66-Br were negatively charged when they were contact-electrified against a Cu surface. The more negative surface potential is, the better the triboelectric performance becomes. The absolute value of surface potential has the order of UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NO}_{2}>$ UiO-66-Br $>$ UiO-66 > UiO-66-NH,which is consistent with the triboelectric performance of the UiO-66-X TENG.
To further understand the charge states of atoms about functionalized UiO-66-X, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was used to probe the electronic effects. Figure $4(\mathsfit{c})$ shows the survey spectrum of UiO-66-X. It is clearly seen that the main elements of UiO-66-X are $\mathrm{C},$ O, and $Z\mathrm{r}$ In addition, the highresolution $\mathrm{Zr}3\mathrm{d}$ of UiO-66-X is shown in Fig.4(d). The curve of the $\mathrm{Zr~}3\mathrm{d}$ region can be deconvoluted into two peaks located at approximately 182.95(Zr $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2})$ and $185.35\ \mathrm{~eV}$ $(\mathrm{Zr}~3\mathrm{d}_{3/2})$ for UiO-66, respectively. After grafted with functional group $\mathrm{\left(-NH_{2},\right.}$ · $\cdot\mathrm{NO}_{2},$ and -Br), the binding energy of Zr $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ are shifted by 0.05, $-0.45,$ and $-0.25\ \mathrm{eV}$ to 185.4, 184.9, and $185.1\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively. These results demonstrate the electron-donating effect of the functional group of ${\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ and the electron-withdrawing character of the functional groups of -Br and $\left.-\mathrm{NO}_{2}\right.$ In particular, the shifts are obvious for the $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ The result shows that the electron-withdrawing ability has the order of U $\mathrm{iO-66-NO_{2}\ >}$ $\mathrm{UiO-66\mathrm{-}B r>U i O-66>U i O-66\mathrm{-}N H_{2},}$ which is in accord with the triboelectric performance of the UiO-66-X TENG.
Based on the surface states model, we speculate that the electronwithdrawing functional group $\left.-\mathrm{NO}_{2}\right.$ causes the decrease of the LUMO of UiO-66, which further leads to the increase of triboelectric properties, and vice versa. Figure 5(a) shows the mechanism of triboelectrification based on the surface state model. The Fermi level $(E_{\mathrm{F}})$ of metal Cu is higher than the neutral level of the surface state $(E_{\mathrm{n}})$ . When they contact each other, fewer electrons from the metal Cu will hop up into the surface of the UiO-66. After grafted with electron-donating functional group · $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2},$ the LUMO decreased, resulting in more charge transfer. Of course, when the electron-donating functional group is replaced by the electron-donating effect of the functional group $\begin{array}{r}{-\mathrm{NH}_{2},}\end{array}$ charge transfer decreases as a result of the higher LUMO. In order to verify our speculation, the density functional theory (DFT） was used to calculate the LUMO (Fig.5(b)). For four isostructural UiO-66-X, both LUMO and highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) are predominantly situated on the organic linkers. For UiO-66, LUMO energy is $-2.32\ \mathrm{eV}.$ 、After grafted with electrondonating functional group $\begin{array}{r}{-\mathrm{NH}_{2},}\end{array}$ the LUMO for UiO $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ increases. However, the LUMO decreases when grafted with electron-withdrawing functional groups of $\left.-\mathrm{NO}_{2}\right.$ and -Br. The order of LUMO energy is UiC $\mathrm{\Delta).66-NO_{2}<U i O_{2}<66-B r<U i O_{-}66<}$ UiO- ${\cdot66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ 、 The theoretical calculation of LUMO is in good agreement with our mechanism assumptions.
## 3 Conclusion
In summary, we have demonstrated a series of UiO-66 films grafted with different functional groups of various polarity $(\mathrm{X}=\mathrm{H},$ ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2},{\mathrm{NO}}_{2},$ and Br) on a FTO substrate as triboelectric materials to investigate the underlying relationships between different functional groups with the electron-donating $\left(\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\right)$ or electronwithdrawing $\left(-\mathrm{NO}_{2}\right.$ and -Br) ability and triboelectric performance of TENG. The $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ TENG shows the highest output property by virtue of the strongest withdrawing ability. The order of triboelectric performance of the UiO-66-X TENG is consistent with the order of the electron-withdrawing ability of the four functional groups. Further, surface potentials, binding energy, and theoretical calculations were tested to study the relation between triboelectric behaviors and functional groups. This work not only provides a strategy to control the output performance of MOF TENG by functionalized MOFs with different functional groups, but also guides the design of high-effciency triboelectric material MOFs for boosting the output performance of TENG.
## 4 Experimental Section
Materials: All chemicals were commercially available and used as received without further purification.
Synthesis of UiO-66-X film: The UiO-66-X film was grown on a FTO-coated glass substrate. The parent UiO-66 film was prepared based on the previous report with some modifications [29].FTO glass ( ${\mathrm{1~cm}}\times2{\mathrm{~cm}} $ ）was washed with deionized water, ethanol, and acetone, respectively, and were dried under air. Then the cleaned FTO glass with FTO facing upward was soaked in 1 mM $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ in dimethylformamide (DMF) solution for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . Zirconium(IV) chloride $\mathrm{(ZrCl_{4})}$ I $(117~\mathrm{mg},0.5~\mathrm{mmol})$ and benzoic acid $(4.5\ \mathrm{g},36.7\ \mathrm{mmol})$ were dissolved in $27~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF, and the mixture was kept in Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ . After cooling the autoclave, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ $(70~\mathrm{mg},0.42~\mathrm{mmol})$ was dissolved into the above solution via sonication, and followed by placing one piece of pretreated FTO glass with the FTO side facing upwards. The autoclave was sealed and heated in an oven at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ to obtain the UiO-66 film on the FTO glass. Finally, FTO glass coated with UiO-66 film was immersed in DMF for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ and methanol (MeOH) for further use. The functionalized UiO-66-X（ $\mathrm{~\boldmath~X~}=\mathrm{~N~H_{2}}.$ $\mathrm{NO}_{2},$ and Br) film was synthesized through the same methods except for replacing the original $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ ligand with the equivalent molar amounts of 2-aminoterephthalic acid $\mathrm{(H_{2}N\mathrm{-}H_{2}B D C)}$ ,2-nitroterephthalic acid $\mathrm{(O_{2}N–H_{2}B D C)}$ , and 2-bromoterephthalic acid $(\mathrm{Br-H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC})$ ·
Fabrication of TENG based on UiO-66-X film: All TENG devices were fabricated by applying a typical vertical contactseparation mode with a simple two-electrode system and the effective area was $1\mathrm{cm}\times1\mathrm{cm}$ The functionalized UiO-66-X film and a piece of copper foil adhered on the acrylic substrate were acted as triboelectric layers. Besides, the copper foil described above also acted as an electrode. Another electrode is FTO, which was coated with UiO-66-X film. Conductive Cu wires led out from the two electrodes were directly connected to an external instrument for electrical measurements.
Materials characterization: XRD spectra were measured by X-ray diffraction (Panalytical Empyrean). FT-IR spectra were recorded on a Bruker-ALPHA spectrophotometer. To prepare FT-IR samples, the functionalized UiO-66-X film was sonicated in MeOH to collect the functionalized UiO-66-X powder. Surface morphology analysis was carried out on a scanning electron microscope (Zeiss Sigma 300). Surface roughness and surface potential distribution were recorded using atomic force microscopy (Asylum research cypher ES). The surface potential distribution was tested using a Trek 347 electrostatic voltmeter.
XPS was measured by Thermo Scientific ESCALAB 250Xi. A system electrometer (Keithley 65l4) was used to record the output voltage and transferred charge of the TENG and a low-noise current density preamplifier (Stanford Research SR570） was used to record the current density signals. All the electrical tests are at $20\%$ relative humidity.
Theoretical calculations: The density functional theory was used to calculate the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital and the highest occupied molecular orbital. The optimized model contained two $\left[\mathrm{Zr_{6}}\right]$ oxygen clusters and one ligand, and the carboxyl group of the clusters is saturated with hydrogen. The software ORCA 5.0 [31] is chosen to optimize by rSCAN-3c method [32]. The dihedral angle of the ligand carboxyl group and $\mathrm{Zr}$ remained fixed during the optimization process. After optimization, the PBE0-D4 [33] and def2-TZVP [34] basis sets were used for energy calculation, and HOMO and LUMO energy and distribution information were obtained. Data processing in the calculation process is carried out using Multiwfn [35] and visual molecular dynamics [36].
